# Flutter & Dart Standards

## Banned patterns

- **Map logic inside `build()`** — No map-related logic (camera, markers, polylines, etc.) inside `build()`. Use state, controllers, or dedicated methods called from lifecycle/callbacks, not from `build()`.
- **Business logic in widgets** — Widgets must only handle UI and delegate to services, repositories, or use cases. No gRPC, parsing, or domain rules in widget files.
- **gRPC calls inside UI** — All gRPC (or any network) calls must be in data/domain layers (repositories, data sources, use cases), never in widgets or `build()`.
- **One giant `providers.dart`** — Split providers by feature or domain (e.g. `auth_providers.dart`, `map_providers.dart`). No single file that registers every provider.
- **Rebuilding GoogleMap every frame** — Do not rebuild the map widget on every frame. Use `const`/`RepaintBoundary`, keys, or move dynamic parts into separate widgets so the map itself is stable and not rebuilt unnecessarily.

## File structure (modular)

- **Feature-first**: `lib/features/<feature>/` with subfolders such as `data/`, `domain/`, `presentation/` (or `ui/`).
- **Core shared code**: `lib/core/` (e.g. network, location, theme, constants).
- **Per-feature**: Keep screens, widgets, and state for a feature inside that feature folder; avoid one huge `screens/` or `widgets/` dump.
- **State**: Prefer feature-scoped providers/bloc files (e.g. `map_providers.dart`, `driver_providers.dart`) instead of a single global providers file.

## Performance

- Keep `build()` methods **cheap**: no heavy computation, no I/O, no creating large objects.
- Use **const** constructors and widgets where possible to reduce rebuilds.
- For maps/lists, use **list view builders** or **lazy loading**; avoid building hundreds of widgets at once.
